[6.026921, "o", "# accounts-db backfills\r\n\r\nSchema migrations and data backfills for the accounts SQLite database (`data/app.db`).\r\n\r\n| file | what it does |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `db.py` | connection helper, schema, fixture generator |\r\n| `migrate.py` | schema migrations, recorded in `schema_migrations` |\r\n| `batcher.py` | batch iteration + batched `UPDATE`s used by every backfill |\r\n| `backfill.py` | backfill jobs (`python3 backfill.py <job>`), progress in `backfill_runs` |\r\n| `checks.py` | post-backfill data checks (exit 0 = pass) |\r\n| `scripts/run_job.sh` | job runner used in production |\r\n| `ops/jobs.toml` | job definitions for the runner |\r\n\r\n## Running jobs\r\n\r\nProduction jobs are started with `scripts/run_job.sh <job>` (job names are in `ops/jobs.toml`).\r\nThe runner applies the job's `max_runtime`, sets `JOB_RUN_ID` and reports the run to the ops\r\nscheduler API. Job output goes to stderr.\r\n\r\n```\r\nscripts/run_job.sh migrate\r\nscripts/run_job.sh users_backfill\r\nscripts/run_job.sh checks\r\n```\r\n\r\nBackfills are idempotent: every run"]
[6.026996, "o", " recomputes all rows, so a failed run is simply re-run.\r\n\r\n## users_contact backfill (migration 0003)\r\n\r\nMigration `0003_users_contact_columns` adds four columns to `users`. The `users` backfill fills\r\nthem from the legacy columns, which must not be modified.\r\n\r\n**email_normalized** — `email` with surrounding whitespace removed, lower-cased. For the domains\r\n`gmail.com` and `googlemail.com` the domain becomes `gmail.com` and the local part drops\r\neverything from the first `+` and all `.` characters (`A.Sharma+news@GoogleMail.com` →\r\n`asharma@gmail.com`). Other domains keep their local part as is (lower-cased).\r\n\r\n**first_name / last_name** — `full_name` with runs of whitespace collapsed and trimmed. Empty →\r\nboth `''`. One word → `first_name` = the word, `last_name` = `''`. Otherwise `last_name` is the\r\nlast word and `first_name` is everything before it (`Asha M. Sharma` → `Asha M.` / `Sharma`).\r\nCase is kept as given.\r\n\r\n**phone_e164** — take the digits of `phone`. The calling code comes from `"]
[6.027027, "o", "country`\r\n(IN 91, US 1, GB 44). If the digits start with the calling code followed by exactly 10 digits, the\r\nnational number is those 10 digits; otherwise, if they are 11 digits starting with a trunk `0`,\r\ndrop the `0`; otherwise the digits are the national number. A 10-digit national number gives\r\n`+<code><national>`; anything else, or a missing phone, gives `NULL`.\r\n\r\n## Checks\r\n\r\n`checks.py` verifies the row count and the legacy columns against `seed_meta`, that the latest\r\n`users` backfill run is complete and covers every row, and that the new columns are well formed.\r\n"]
[6.028708, "o", "\"\"\"Backfill jobs: compute derived columns for existing rows after a schema migration.\r\n\r\nUsage: python3 backfill.py <job>     (in production, via scripts/run_job.sh)\r\n\"\"\"\r\nimport os\r\n"]
[6.028774, "o", "import sys\r\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\r\n\r\nimport batcher\r\nimport db\r\nfrom migrate import migrate\r\n\r\n\r\ndef transform_user(row):\r\n    \"\"\"users_contact backfill (migration 0003): see README.md for the column rules.\"\"\"\r\n    raise NotImplementedError(\"users_contact transform not written yet\")\r\n\r\n"]
[6.028815, "o", "\r\nJOBS = {\r\n    \"users\": {\r\n        \"table\": \"users\",\r\n        \"columns\": (\"email\", \"full_name\", \"phone\", \"country\"),\r\n        \"transform\": transform_user,\r\n    },\r\n}\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _now():\r\n    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec=\"seconds\")\r\n\r\n\r\ndef run_job(name, conn=None, batch_size=500):\r\n    spec = JOBS[name]\r\n    conn = conn or db.connect()\r\n    migrate(conn)\r\n    run_id = os.environ.get(\"JOB_RUN_ID\") or f\"manual-{os.getpid()}-{_now()}\"\r\n    with conn:\r\n        conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO backfill_runs (run_id, job, started_at, status) VALUES (?, ?, ?, 'running')\",\r\n"]
[6.028854, "o", "                     (run_id, name, _now()))\r\n\r\n    def progress(done):\r\n        conn.execute(\"UPDATE backfill_runs SET rows_done = ? WHERE run_id = ?\", (done, run_id))\r\n\r\n    try:\r\n        done = batcher.run(conn, spec[\"table\"], spec[\"columns\"], spec[\"transform\"], size=batch_size,\r\n                           on_progress=progress)\r\n    except BaseException:\r\n        with conn:\r\n            conn.execute(\"UPDATE backfill_runs SET status = 'failed', finished_at = ? WHERE run_id = ?\",\r\n                         (_now(), run_id))\r\n        raise\r\n    with conn:\r\n        conn.execute(\"UPDATE backfill_runs SET status = 'complete', finished_at = ?, rows_done = ? \"\r\n                     \"WHERE run_id = ?\", (_now(), done, run_id))\r\n    print(f\"{name}: backfill complete, {done} rows (run {run_id})\")\r\n"]
[6.028879, "o", "    return done\r\n\r\n\r\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\r\n    if len(sys.argv) != 2 or sys.argv[1] not in JOBS:\r\n        sys.exit(f\"usage: backfill.py <{'|'.join(JOBS)}>\")\r\n    run_job(sys.argv[1])\r\n"]
[6.030437, "o", "#!/usr/bin/env bash\r\n# Run a job from ops/jobs.toml under its runtime budget and report the run to the ops scheduler API.\r\n# usage: scripts/run_job.sh <job>\r\nset -uo pipefail\r\nROOT=$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")/..\" && pwd)\r\nJOB=${1:?usage: scripts/run_job.sh <job>}\r\nOPS_API=${OPS_API:-http://127.0.0.1:9300}\r\n\r\nmapfile -t CFG < <(python3 - \"$ROOT/ops/jobs.toml\" \"$JOB\" <<'PY'\r\nimport sys, tomllib\r\njobs = tomllib.load(open(sys.argv[1], \"rb\")).get(\"jobs\", {})\r\njob = jobs.get(sys.argv[2])\r\nif job is None:\r\n    sys.exit(f\"unknown job {sys.argv[2]!r}; known: {', '.join(sorted(jobs))}\")\r\nprint(job[\"command\"]); print(int(job[\"max_runtime\"])); print(int(job.get(\"kill_grace\", 5)))\r\nPY\r\n) || exit 2\r\n[ ${#CFG[@]} -eq 3 ] || { echo \"run_job: bad config for $JOB\" >&2; exit 2; }\r\nCMD=${CFG[0]} MAX=${CFG[1]} GRACE=${CFG[2]}\r\nRUN_ID=\"$JOB-$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)-$$\"\r\n\r\nreport() {  # report <event> <status> <rc> <elapsed>\r\n  curl -fsS --max-time 3 -X POST \"$OPS_API/v1/runs\" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\r\n    -d \"{\\\"run_id\\\":"]
[6.030514, "o", "\\\"$RUN_ID\\\",\\\"job\\\":\\\"$JOB\\\",\\\"event\\\":\\\"$1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"$2\\\",\\\"rc\\\":$3,\\\"elapsed\\\":$4,\\\"max_runtime\\\":$MAX}\" \\\r\n    >/dev/null 2>&1 || echo \"run_job: warning: could not report $1 to $OPS_API\" >&2\r\n}\r\n\r\necho \"[run_job] $JOB: starting run $RUN_ID (max_runtime=${MAX}s)\" >&2\r\nreport start running 0 0\r\nSTART=$(date +%s)\r\ncd \"$ROOT\"\r\nJOB_RUN_ID=$RUN_ID timeout --kill-after=\"$GRACE\" \"$MAX\" bash -c \"$CMD\"\r\nRC=$?\r\nELAPSED=$(( $(date +%s) - START ))\r\n\r\nif [ $RC -eq 124 ] || [ $RC -eq 137 ]; then\r\n  echo \"[run_job] $JOB: exceeded max_runtime (${MAX}s), killed after ${ELAPSED}s\" >&2\r\n  report finish killed $RC $ELAPSED\r\n  exit 124\r\nelif [ $RC -ne 0 ]; then\r\n  echo \"[run_job] $JOB: failed with exit $RC after ${ELAPSED}s\" >&2\r\n  report finish failed $RC $ELAPSED\r\nelse\r\n  echo \"[run_job] $JOB: ok in ${ELAPSED}s\" >&2\r\n  report finish ok 0 $ELAPSED\r\nfi\r\nexit $RC\r\n"]
